💭 A 🤝 or a 👐 frees whatever its statement made before it.
💭
💭 Expected: three lines that say "arrived whole".
💭 What happens: the third says that the text was freed. The code for 🤝 and 👐 releases
💭 the statement's temporary objects after each of its two sides
💭 (ASTBinaryOperator::generateLogical), and those are all the temporaries that the
💭 statement has made so far, not only the ones the two sides made. So an object that
💭 was made earlier in the statement, as an earlier argument or as the receiver, is
💭 freed before the call that was to get it. Here the text has been written over by
💭 the allocator by the time it is read. With an interpolated text in its place, or a
💭 fresh object that holds a text, the program is a segmentation fault. 👐 does the same.
💭
💭 Putting the test into a variable first is fine (the second call), and so is a 🤝
💭 that comes before the object is made.
